@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility with the original xwpe

@cindex compatibility
@cindex differences from xwpe 1.4

This chapter lists what xwpe now does differently from the xwpe of the
1990s, where a user of the original would notice it.  Each entry says
what the original did, what xwpe does now, and why.

@section While the debugger answers at a stop

@table @asis
@item Ctrl-C while the debugger answers
@cindex Ctrl-C, while gdb answers
The original asked, in a box, whether to quit the debugger when
@kbd{Ctrl-C} was typed while it read the debugger's answer.  Under gdb,
pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, @kbd{Ctrl-C} now stops waiting for the
answer, as @key{Esc}
does, and the status row says which command the debugger did not answer
(or could not be given, when the command was too long for it, or that it
answered with another prompt of its own);
Debug > Quit ends the session.  The box is gone: the editor keeps the
keyboard while the debugger answers, at a stop, while the debugger starts
or when a run or a step goes out, so there is no moment where only a box
could get you out.

@item Keys typed while the debugger answers
@cindex keys, while gdb answers
The original passed every key typed while it read the debugger's answer
on to the debugger.  Under gdb, pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, keys
typed then are the editor's: text
goes into the file, menus open, and a debugger command waits for the
answer or is refused with the waiting line.  A key that edits acts at
once, so it may act before the step typed just before it has stopped.
Keys that reached the debugger behind the editor's back were how an
answer could be lost or a letter typed into the source.
@end table

@section When the editor ends during a debug session

@table @asis
@item The debugger at the editor's end
@cindex debugger, at the editor's end
The original sent the debugger signal 7 when it ended during a session,
which on Linux is SIGBUS: the debugger died with a core file and could
leave its program running.  xwpe now ends it as Debug > Quit does: told
to quit at its prompt, or sent SIGTERM while its program runs, and
killed only if it is still there two seconds later, so the debugger ends
its program and leaves no core file.
@end table

@section Where the program under the debugger writes

@table @asis
@item The program's terminal
@cindex debugger, terminal of the program
The original terminal editor refused a session unless its own input was
a terminal, and sent the program's output there, over the editor's
screen.  xwpe gives the program a
pseudoterminal of its own and reads its output through it, so a session
starts even when the editor has no terminal, as when it is started from a
desktop launcher.  The editor's terminal is used only when no
pseudoterminal could be opened.
@end table

@section Keys

@table @asis
@item F11 and F12 under X
@cindex F11, F12
The original read the key symbols of @key{F11} and @key{F12} as the Sun
keyboard's Stop and Again keys, which they also are, so a PC keyboard's
@key{F11} quit the editor and @key{F12} redid.  They are now the keys
@key{F11} and @key{F12} on every display, with Shift, Ctrl and Alt; the
Borland key set gives them no action, so they do nothing, as they always
did in a terminal.  One key gives one action on every display, and
quitting on a key no menu names surprised users.

@item Key reports in a terminal
@cindex key reports, unknown
@cindex escape sequences, unknown
The original typed the tail of an escape sequence it did not know as
text, so a modified key a newer terminal sends (a Ctrl-Shift chord under
xterm's modifyOtherKeys or the kitty keyboard protocol) put digits and
letters in the file.  A report is now read as the key it is, or dropped
whole, however long it is, so that no part of it is typed.

@item Unambiguous keys in a terminal
@cindex modifyOtherKeys
@cindex kitty keyboard protocol
@cindex Ctrl-H, Ctrl-I, Ctrl-M
@cindex Ctrl-Shift, letters
The original had only the bytes of the legacy keyboard, where Esc waits
to see whether a key follows it, Ctrl-Shift-@var{letter} is
Ctrl-@var{letter}, and Ctrl-H, Ctrl-I, Ctrl-M and Ctrl-[ are the bytes of
Backspace, Tab, Enter and Esc.  xwpe asks the terminal for xterm's
modifyOtherKeys and the kitty keyboard protocol, and gives them back when
it ends, is suspended or crashes, and whenever it hands the terminal to a
program.  In a terminal that knows either, Esc acts at once,
Ctrl-Shift-@var{letter} is a key of its own (in the Borland key set it
does what Ctrl-@var{letter} does), and Ctrl-H, Ctrl-I, Ctrl-M and Ctrl-[
are keys of their own that do what Backspace, Tab, Enter and Esc do.  A
terminal that knows neither keeps sending the legacy bytes, read as
before.  The reason is that a terminal able to tell these keys apart
should not lose the difference.

@item Keys typed to a program run from the editor
@cindex Run, keys for the program
@cindex Ctrl-D, end of input
In the original, the keys typed while a program ran in the editor reached
it as the bytes the terminal gave.  They now reach it as its own terminal
expects: Ctrl-D ends its input, Esc, Tab and the Ctrl keys are their
bytes, and the cursor and function keys are the xterm sequences (the
original dropped them).  Ctrl-C still stops the program, in every form a
terminal sends it, so it is no longer typed into the program byte by byte.

@item Ctrl-\ in a terminal and under X
@cindex Ctrl-backslash
The original gave Shift-Tab the code 28, which is also the byte Ctrl-\
sends, so Ctrl-\ moved the focus back in a dialog or the file manager as
Shift-Tab does.  Shift-Tab is now a code of its own and Ctrl-\ does
nothing there, because one key should not act as another.

@item Ctrl and Alt chords by the key's place
@cindex Alt-Shift, letters
@cindex keyboard layouts
Under X11 and Wayland, Alt-Shift with a letter was a code nothing read,
while a terminal selected the window of that number.  It now selects
window 10 and up everywhere.  Ctrl and Alt chords on a key that types no
Latin letter are read by the letter on the key's place in the first Latin
layout of the keyboard, so Ctrl-S saves on a Cyrillic or Greek layout too;
a Latin layout (German, French, Dvorak) keeps its own letters.  The reason
is one decision for every display.

@item The second key style is Modern
@cindex CUA style
@cindex Modern style
@cindex key styles
The original's second style, kept in 1.4 to 1.6 as CUA (@kbd{Alt-F2} saves,
@kbd{F2} opens a file), is now the Modern style: @kbd{Ctrl-S} saves,
@kbd{Ctrl-O} opens, @kbd{Ctrl-Z} and @kbd{Ctrl-Y} undo and redo.  A key it
kept does what it did; the keys that now do something else (@kbd{F5},
@kbd{Ctrl-F5}, @kbd{F2}, @kbd{Ctrl-F6} among them) and the keys that do nothing
are two generated lists in the key reference.  The first start after the
upgrade turns an option file of the old style into the new one, once, and says
so on the status row.  Few people used the old style in a terminal, where most
of its keys did not arrive, and a third style would have left a dead one in the
dialog.

@item Control letters in the Modern style
@cindex Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N, Ctrl-B, Ctrl-F
@cindex Ctrl-K, chords
The original gave Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N, Ctrl-B, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E, Ctrl-T,
Ctrl-Z, Ctrl-Y and Ctrl-D to the cursor and to deleting, and Ctrl-O started a
prefix.  The Modern style gives those letters to the commands they name in
other editors (Ctrl-F finds, Ctrl-A selects all) and has no Ctrl-O or Ctrl-G
prefix, so Ctrl-K is the only one left: a letter after it is a WordStar block
command, and a control letter after it is a chord of its own, not the letter.
The Borland style is unchanged.  A key means one thing in a style.

@item Which keys to use, asked once
@cindex first run
@cindex --keys
@cindex XWPE_KEYS
The original had one key set.  A new profile (no option file and no
configuration folder) is now asked which keys it wants, in a box where Enter
picks Modern; the answer is written to the option file at once.  An existing
installation is never asked, so no one's keys change under them.  @samp{--keys
borland} and @samp{--keys modern} on the command line, or @env{XWPE_KEYS},
choose the keys for one run and never write them to the option file, and a
run whose input is not a terminal is never asked.  A new profile started from a
script or as another program's editor would otherwise stop on a question
nobody can answer.

@item Keypad Enter in a terminal
@cindex keypad Enter
The original ignored the keypad's @key{Enter} in a terminal that sends it
as its own key (xterm in application keypad mode).  It is now Enter,
as it is under X11 and Wayland.

@item Alt-Delete in a terminal
@cindex Alt-Delete, in a terminal
The original read Esc then Delete in a terminal as Shift-Delete and cut
the marked block.  It is now @kbd{Alt-Delete} on every display, which
copies the block (to the system selection on X11 and Wayland, to the
editor's clipboard in a terminal) and leaves it in the file.

@item Alt-Insert and Alt-Delete under X
@cindex Alt-Insert, under X
The original had a paste and a copy of their own for the X selection.
They now run Paste and Copy, so they behave as @kbd{Ctrl-V} and
@kbd{Ctrl-Insert} do on every display, with five differences under X:
@kbd{Alt-Insert} pastes the editor's clipboard when no application owns
the selection (it pasted nothing); after whole lines were copied it pastes
them as whole lines (it joined the last one to the next line); a window
closed to changes, such as the clipboard viewer, refuses it before
anything changes; a block that ends at the start of a line is copied
without that last line break, as @kbd{Ctrl-Insert} copies it; and
@kbd{Alt-Delete} in the clipboard viewer copies nothing, as
@kbd{Ctrl-Insert} there (it gave the viewer's text to the X selection
again).  @kbd{Ctrl-V} and @kbd{Alt-Insert} take the newest copy on X11 and
Wayland: another application's @kbd{Ctrl-C} text wins over the editor's
own clipboard when it came later, and the editor's clipboard wins over
another application's primary selection.  The editor used to paste its own
text for as long as it still owned the primary selection.  The reason
is one clipboard path for every display, so no display has a copy of its
own to get wrong.
@end table

@section What arrives while a box is open

@table @asis
@item Output, stops and answers under a dialog or a menu
@cindex dialog, output while open
The original did nothing in the background: while a build ran, the
debugger started or the program ran, the editor waited, so no menu or
dialog could be open when output or a stop arrived.  xwpe keeps the
keyboard meanwhile, so a box, the menu bar, a prefix such as
@kbd{Ctrl-G} or the User Screen can be open when they arrive; what
arrives then is kept and drawn when it closes (@pxref{Interface tour}).
The same holds for the program a run starts and for what the loop brings
(the build's output, a reply, a language server's news): the original had
no such callbacks, and here none draws while a box or a run has the screen.
A box drawn over is a box you can no longer read.
@end table

@section Where the boxes are drawn

@table @asis
@item The About and System Info boxes
@cindex About box, place
@cindex System Info box, place
The original drew the About box at a fixed place, 8 columns in and 3 rows
down, and the System Info box 4 columns in and 3 rows down, on every
screen.  xwpe centres both between the menu bar and the status line, also
after the screen changes size, so a small screen shows them whole and they
never cover the status line.  The reason: at a fixed place the About box
ran over the status line on a screen of fewer than 22 rows.
@end table

@section Undo and breakpoints

@table @asis
@item Enter with auto-indent
@cindex undo, auto-indent
The original needed two @kbd{Ctrl-U} for one @key{Return} with
auto-indent (the indent, then the line break), and its Redo indented the
line a second time.  It is now one step each way, because one key is one
action.

@item Tab with auto-indent
@cindex undo, Tab
The original needed two @kbd{Ctrl-U} for one @key{Tab} with auto-indent:
the first put the indent away and left the blanks behind the cursor
erased, the second gave the blanks back.  One @kbd{Ctrl-U} now gives the
line back as it was and one @kbd{Ctrl-R} indents it again, because one key
is one action.

@item Backspace after the first character of a line
@cindex Backspace, auto-indent
The original with auto-indent on re-indented the line when @key{Backspace}
was pressed right after the first visible character, so the character
went together with the indent.  @key{Backspace} now takes that character
alone, and re-indents only when the cursor is among the leading blanks, as
the manual says.

@item Backspace in an indent
@cindex undo, Backspace
The original needed up to three @kbd{Ctrl-U} for one @key{Backspace} that
re-indented a line with auto-indent (its tabs turned into blanks, the
blanks put in, the indent shortened).  One @kbd{Ctrl-U} now gives the line
back as it was, because one key is one action.

@item Ctrl-K I and Ctrl-K U
@cindex undo, block indent
The original took a block's indent or unindent back one line per
@kbd{Ctrl-U}, so a block of thirty lines needed thirty, and a small Num.@:
Undo kept only the last few lines.  One @kbd{Ctrl-U} now gives the whole
block back and one @kbd{Ctrl-R} shifts it again, because one command is
one action.

@item Ctrl-O U, M and H
@cindex undo, help file marks
The original needed two @kbd{Ctrl-U} to take a help file's button,
highlight or headline marks away (the end mark, then the start mark).  One
@kbd{Ctrl-U} now takes both away and one @kbd{Ctrl-R} puts both back,
because one command is one action.

@item Change All
@cindex undo, Change All
The original took Search > Replace's Change All back one replacement per
@kbd{Ctrl-U}, and Num.@: Undo kept only the last few of them, so the
others could not be taken back at all.  One @kbd{Ctrl-U} now gives
back every replacement the command made, and one @kbd{Ctrl-R} makes them
all again, because one command is one action.  Num.@: Undo counts
actions, never their pieces.

@item The block after Undo of an action of several pieces
@cindex undo, block marks
The original's @kbd{Ctrl-U} of each piece of such an action marked what
that piece gave back.  One @kbd{Ctrl-U} or @kbd{Ctrl-R} of the whole action
now marks all the text it brought, from its first byte to its last; when
it brought no single run of text, @kbd{Ctrl-U} gives back the block you
had before the action and @kbd{Ctrl-R} the block the action left, because
the block should show what the key did, never only its last piece.

@item Undo of Ctrl-Y
The original marked the restored line as a block and left the cursor at
its start.  Undo now puts the cursor and the block back where they were
before the deletion, so a block the deletion did not touch survives.

@item The block's end on a deleted line
When the line holding the end of the block was deleted, the original moved
the end to the end of the line above.  It now goes to the start of the line
that took the deleted line's place (to the end of the file when the last
line went), as the block's beginning always did, so both ends of a block
follow the same rule.

@item Ctrl-Y on the only line
The original cleared the only line of a file without recording it, so
@kbd{Ctrl-U} could not bring it back.  It is now undoable like any other
deleted line.

@item Breakpoints on deleted or joined lines
@cindex breakpoints, undo
The original removed the breakpoint of a deleted line (@kbd{Ctrl-Y}, or
@key{Backspace} joining it to the line above) for good, and kept a
breakpoint on its line number when lines were added above it by a wrapped
line or by a tool's rewrite of the file.  The breakpoint now comes back
with Undo, and stays on its code when lines move, so the program stops
where the margin shows.  During a debugging session the debugger is told
of the move too, at once at a stop and after its answer while it is
answering, so a key that moves a breakpoint is never refused for it.

@item Block copy, move and delete
@cindex breakpoints, block operations
@cindex undo, block operations
The original left every breakpoint on its line number when a block was
copied, moved or pasted above it, and dropped the breakpoints of a deleted
block for good; a Search > Replace that made its line wrap left the
breakpoint below on the wrapped tail.  Now a breakpoint stays on its code:
lines a copy or a paste adds push it down, a moved line takes its
breakpoint along (a block that starts at column 0 owns its first line, so
that line's breakpoint moves too when the block is dropped at the start of
a line), and Undo and Redo of a block delete bring the breakpoints of its
lines back and take them away again.  A copy carries no breakpoint.  As in
the original, a breakpoint on a line whose text is merged into another
line goes, and Undo does not bring that one back: the last line of a
block that ends inside a line, when the block is deleted or moved, and
the first line of a block that starts at column 0, when it is dropped
inside a line.

@item Undo of a block moved inside its own line
The original's @kbd{Ctrl-U} of a block moved to an earlier place on the same
line put it past the end of the line, and @kbd{Ctrl-R} of one moved to a
later place put it too far.  Both now give back the line as it was.  A
block copied or moved into a line as wide as the window, which then
wraps, was marked as if the line had not wrapped, so its @kbd{Ctrl-U} took
away characters of yours beside it; it is now marked on the rows it is on.

@item A block operation without memory
@cindex out of memory, block operations
The original stopped a block move, copy, paste or delete that ran out of
memory with a low-memory box that offered to save.  An action of several
pieces that could not record its Undo showed the same box and made the
change anyway, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} could not take it back.  Now a block
operation, an action of several pieces (Return, Tab or Backspace with
auto-indent, Ctrl-K I and U, Ctrl-O U, M and H, Change All), and the
@kbd{Ctrl-U} or @kbd{Ctrl-R} of any change, are made whole or not at all:
refused, the text, the block, the breakpoints and both Undo and Redo are as
they were, and the status row says
@samp{Out of memory: the block move was not made, the text is
unchanged}.  Change All that asks before each replacement stops at the one
it cannot make, as @key{Esc} would, and says so.  A file that cannot be
read for want of memory is not opened, so it cannot be saved over as an
empty window.

@item Where a moved or pasted block wraps
A line that a block move, copy, paste or delete makes, together with the
rows its soft-wrapped line goes on to, is wrapped where the file reader
wraps a long line, at the last blank or dash that fits; the original
wrapped it as its typing did, a word earlier.  The file written is the
same.

@item Change Case
@cindex undo, change case
The original changed the case of a block (@kbd{Ctrl-K D}) without
recording it, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} could not take it back.  It is now one undo
step, with the breakpoints and the block left where they were.

@item Formats, renames and accepted Edits
@cindex breakpoints, format
@cindex undo, format
The original had no tool that rewrote the whole file.  Here a format, a
rename, a code action and an accepted assistant Edit change only the lines
that differ, and a line that only changed its indentation counts as the same
line.  A breakpoint, the block and the colours stay on their code when the
tool adds, removes or re-indents lines, and one @kbd{Ctrl-U} gives back the
whole change.  Too different a rewrite (more than about two thousand lines
added or removed) is taken as one change to the lines in the middle, and
there breakpoints stay on their line numbers.

@item A file read into the middle of a window
@cindex undo, Block Read
@cindex breakpoints, Block Read
The original split the line at the cursor and put the file's lines in by
hand (@kbd{Ctrl-K R}), recording nothing: @kbd{Ctrl-U} took back whatever
you had typed before, not the insert, the window did not read as modified
because of it, and the breakpoints below stayed on their line numbers.  The
insert is now one undo step, the window reads as modified, and the
breakpoints below move down with their lines, as they do for a paste.

@item The Stack and Watches windows
@cindex undo, Stack and Watches
The original recorded the rows it took out of the Stack window, so
@key{Edit}, @key{Undo} there put back a row of an earlier listing.  What
the debugger printed is not yours to undo: those windows are rewritten at
every stop, and Undo in them says there is nothing to undo.

@item A stack that gets shorter
The original left every other row of the old listing in the Stack window
when the stack lost two or more frames.  The window now shows the new
listing and nothing else.
@end table

@section Editing keys

@table @asis
@item Ctrl-T at the end of the text
@cindex Ctrl-T, end of the text
The original deleted the last line break of the file when @kbd{Ctrl-T}
was pressed at the end of the text, and a second @kbd{Ctrl-T} put the
cursor at the start of the last line.  As in Turbo Pascal and Borland C++,
@kbd{Ctrl-T} there now deletes nothing and the cursor stays: there is no
word and no line to its right.

@item Ctrl-Z at the end of a line
@cindex Ctrl-Z, end of a line
The original recorded an undo step of nothing for @kbd{Ctrl-Z} or
@kbd{Ctrl-O Y} at the end of a line, so the next @kbd{Ctrl-U} took back
nothing, and on an empty last row it deleted the row.  It now deletes
nothing and records nothing there, as the Borland editors do, so
@kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back the change before it.

@item A blank typed at the right margin
The original kept a blank typed at the margin at the end of its row and
wrapped what followed.  The blank now starts the next row with what is
typed after it, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back exactly what was typed, in one
place.  The file written is the same.

@item Rows as wide as Max. Columns
@cindex Max. Columns, row width
@cindex word wrap, typing
The original wrapped typed text two columns before Max.@: Columns, could
lose a character typed where a row had to wrap or cut one outside ASCII
in two, showed a line exactly Max.@: Columns long as a full row with an
empty row below it, and could cut a long line inside a character outside
ASCII when it read a file.  A row now holds Max.@: Columns bytes, the line
break after them, and typing wraps at the next byte, as the manual says:
the row is cut after the last blank or dash that leaves room, else before
the margin, never inside a character, and what you type goes in whole,
on the row below when the cut falls at or before it.  A line read from a
file is cut the same way; a blank in the first two columns counts too.
@key{Backspace} that joins a line which exactly fills what the row above
has left takes the whole line and its line break.  Max.@: Columns counts
bytes: a character outside ASCII takes two to four.  A file whose last
line is a multiple of Max.@: Columns long and has no final line break
gains one when saved, as every other file without one already does.  One
part of the editor now decides how wide a row is, by the manual's own
rule.  A replacement (Search > Replace) or a completion longer than
Max.@: Columns goes in whole over as many rows as it needs, and
@kbd{Ctrl-U} takes it out again; the replace that follows goes on behind
it, also when it wrapped.

@item Overwrite over a character outside ASCII
@cindex overwrite, multibyte characters
The original wrote a character typed in overwrite mode over one byte, so
typed over an @samp{é} or a @samp{€} it left the rest of that character
behind as bytes that are no text.  It now replaces the whole character
under the cursor, and @kbd{Ctrl-U} gives that character back whole.

@item The cursor between the bytes of a character
@cindex cursor, between the bytes of a character
@cindex Page Up, Page Down, Goto Line, Ctrl-Y, screen column
The original kept the byte index of the cursor when a key changed the
row.  After @key{Page Up}, @key{Page Down}, Goto Line or @kbd{Ctrl-Y} the
cursor could stand between the two bytes of an @samp{é}, and a letter
typed or @key{Delete} then split it and left bytes that are no text.  On a
row with tabs the same byte index was also another screen column.  Now
every key, the mouse, Undo and the markers put the cursor and the block
marks on a character, never between its bytes, and @key{Page Up},
@key{Page Down}, @kbd{Ctrl-Y} and Goto Line keep the screen column, as
@key{Up} and @key{Down} always did.  @key{Backspace} at the start of the
row below a row that goes on without a line break takes the whole last
character of that row, not its last byte.  After @kbd{Ctrl-K Y} on a block
inside one row the original kept the cursor on its own row and took the
block's column there, which on another row could be between the bytes of a
character; the cursor now goes to where the deleted block began, on the
block's row.  When @key{Delete},
@key{Backspace} or a join makes one character out of stray bytes (a
Latin-1 file), the cursor stands before that character.  The reason: a
character of several bytes is one place, and a position that is not a
place is how a file stops being text.  Latin-1 bytes that happen to form a
UTF-8 character (@samp{Ã} followed by a no-break space) are one character
to the cursor, as they are to the screen.  The places the editor jumps to
on its own (a compiler's column, a click in Messages, a bracket match)
land on a character too, and a Replace whose regular expression matches
only part of a character leaves that match as it is and says so on the
status row.

@item A Shift selection that goes back past its start
@cindex Shift selection, back past its start
The original moved only the end of the block the cursor stood on, so a
Shift key that took the cursor back past the place the selection started
from (@kbd{Shift-End}, then @kbd{Shift-Home}) left an empty block, and
nothing was selected.  The block now runs from that place to the cursor,
on whichever side of it the cursor is.  The reason: a selection is the
text between where it started and the cursor.

@item Text that is not UTF-8
@cindex Latin-1 text
@cindex UTF-8, bytes that are no character
The original showed every byte as one Latin-1 character.  Text is now
UTF-8, so an @samp{é} written in Latin-1, or the first byte of a
character cut in two, is shown as @samp{?} in a window and as the
replacement mark @samp{�} in a dialog field.  Such a byte counts as one
byte: the cursor steps over it, @key{Left}, @key{Backspace} and
@key{Delete} take it alone, in a window and in a dialog field, a letter
typed over it in overwrite mode replaces it alone, and saving writes it
back as it was.  Going back takes what going forward takes, so a key
never takes two glyphs the screen shows apart.  Bytes of the right shape
that are still no UTF-8 (an overlong form such as @samp{C0 80}, a
surrogate, a lead byte above @samp{F4}) are one glyph too, shown the same
way, so the cursor, the mouse and the keys agree on where it starts and
ends.

@item The block Undo marks around a line break
@cindex undo, block marks
The original's @kbd{Ctrl-U} of a deletion that took a line break marked
the text it gave back up to a column past the end of its row, and a
@kbd{Ctrl-K C} or @kbd{Ctrl-K V} of that block followed by @kbd{Ctrl-U}
could lose a line.  The block now ends at the start of the next row, the place
just after the line break, and after any key no mark, and no place a
Shift key selects from, is past the end of its row.  So a Shift selection
made after typing at the end of a line in overwrite mode no longer lets
@kbd{Ctrl-O H}, @kbd{U} or @kbd{M} put a byte behind the line break,
where Save lost it.

@item The tenth marker
@cindex markers, ten
The original kept markers 0 to 8 and let marker 9 share its place with the
search highlight, so it moved whenever another marker was set.  There are
now ten markers, 0 to 9, each with a place of its own, as the Borland
editors have ten bookmarks.

@item Commands in a read-only window
@cindex read-only window, commands
The original let the block and line commands change a file opened
read-only and the clipboard viewer: @kbd{Ctrl-K I} indented it,
@kbd{Ctrl-O E} added a line, Search > Replace replaced.  Those commands
are now refused there and the status row says so, because the window
says it cannot be changed.

@item Ctrl-O E
The original put the help file's end-of-page mark on an empty row without
a line break, so the screen showed it on a line of its own while the file
written ran it into the start of the next line.  The mark now has a line of
its own in the file too, and one @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes that line back.

@item Line endings of a saved file
@cindex line endings
@cindex CR LF
@cindex carriage return
The original took a file with any carriage return in it for a DOS file,
dropped every carriage return and wrote the whole file with CR LF.  A file
that had one CR LF line among LF lines came back with every line changed,
and a carriage return inside a line was lost.  A file is now CRLF only when
every line break in it is CR LF; any other file is LF, and each carriage
return in it is a byte of the text, shown as @samp{^M} and written back
where it was.  A file with no line break at all (empty, or one line) takes
the style of the new files, which the @samp{Data} key of the options file
sets.  Saving must not change lines you did not edit.

@item A NUL byte in a file
@cindex NUL byte
The original wrote nothing of a line after a NUL byte in it, line break
included, so the line ran into the next one when saved.  A line cannot hold
a NUL byte: it is left out when the file is read, and the rest of the line
and its line break are kept.  The status row says how many were left out,
Block > Read says the same, and the first Save of such a file asks before
it writes the file without them (No leaves the file on disk as it is).

@item Closing a window whose save fails
@cindex close, failed save
The original closed the window after Yes to @samp{Do you want to save File ?}
even when the file could not be written, and the edits were lost.  The
window now stays open with its text, and Quit stops at it.

@item Selection, Trim Auto-indent and Keep Final newline
@cindex selection mode
@cindex final line break
The original never let typing touch a marked block, left the blanks
@key{Return} had indented a line on that line when the cursor went away,
and always ended a saved file on a line break.  Three boxes of
@samp{Options @result{} Editor} change each of these, and all three are
clear until you tick them: with @samp{SelEction blocK} typing replaces the
selection, with @samp{Trim Auto-indent} the unused indent goes, with
@samp{Keep Final newline} a file saved ends as it was read.  The reason is
that other editors work this way and the original's way is still the
default.

@item The @samp{Data} key of the options file
@cindex options file, Data
The original read the number after @samp{Data} into the kind of every new
window, so a value that was not a text file's kind (70, say, in a
@file{xwperc} of a folder you cloned) made the editor fail at start or open
windows that took no keys.  The key now only says how new files break their
lines (109, the original's MS-DOS file, is CR LF; anything else is LF) and is
written back as 109 or 110.
@end table

@section Short commands

@table @asis
@item Print, manual pages and compressed help files
@cindex print command, time limit
@cindex man, time limit
The original ran the print command, @command{man} and @command{gunzip}
through the shell and waited for them with no limit, the editor frozen
meanwhile.  Each now has a time limit (a minute to print, 30 seconds for
a page or an unpack), the window is drawn again while it runs, and one
that takes longer than a second is named on the status row.  On X11 and
Wayland, @key{Esc} ends it at once and the status row says
@samp{Esc: stopped waiting for} the command; nothing else follows, so no
``Not Installed'' box after Print and no ``No manual entry'' window after
Help, since you chose not to wait.  The keys typed before @key{Esc} are
still read by the editor afterwards.  A check the editor makes by itself
(the version of your Java before a Scala session, the desktop's font) does
not take your @key{Esc}: it stays for what you were doing.  In a terminal
the keyboard waits for the editor until the command ends.  A command that
hung used to leave no way out but killing the editor.
@end table

@section Commands a project folder names

@table @asis
@item The print command, compilers and debugger of a folder's xwperc or a project file
@cindex xwperc, commands from a project folder
@cindex project file, commands from
The original read an @file{xwperc} in the folder it was started in as
the whole configuration, and ran its print command, compiler lines and
debugger, and a project file's @code{CMP}, @code{CMPFLAGS},
@code{LDFLAGS} and @code{install:} lines, with no question (a Scala
project's @file{.bsp} build server is newer than the original, and is
treated the same way).  An @code{Exedir}, @code{FILES} or @code{LIBNAME}
value starting with @samp{-} or @samp{@@} was handed to the compiler as
an option or a response file; it is now passed as @file{./}@var{path}.
Now the first time such a command is about to run (a compile, a link, a
print, an install, a debugger start or a build server start) and
differs from your own setting, xwpe shows it and asks @samp{Run it},
@samp{Use mine} or @samp{Cancel}, and remembers the answer for that
folder and command in your state folder (@pxref{Folder commands}).  The
reason: the commands a folder's files name for a compile, a link, a
print, an install, a debugger start or a build-server start now ask
once, and a path a folder value hands to a program is never read as an
option or a response file.  Run and Make still run the folder's own
program and @file{Makefile}; the next entry covers their
@code{Arguments}.

Not covered yet, and planned: a folder's @code{IncludePath} widening
what the assistant may read.

@item Run, Make, Execute and the language servers
@cindex Run, folder Arguments
@cindex Execute, file names
The original joined the program's path, the source's name and the
@code{Arguments} into one shell line for Run, handed the selected file's
name to the shell for File > Execute, and gave @command{make} the
folder's @code{Arguments} unasked; a file named @file{a;touch x;.c} also
ran @samp{touch x}, a name with a quote ran nothing, and a folder's
@samp{; touch x} in @code{Arguments} ran.  Now the program and every
file name reach the shell as separate words, Execute runs the file as
@file{./}@var{name} with no shell, a folder's @code{Arguments} are shown
and asked about once per folder and then reach the program and
@command{make} as words, and your own @code{Arguments} stay your shell
text as before.  The language servers are newer than the original;
@command{rust-analyzer} and Metals, which run the folder's build when a
file opens, ask once per folder before they start.  The reason: what a
folder decides about a program the editor starts is shown before it
runs, and no file name is ever read as a command.

@item A debug session's start-up files and the values it sends the debugger
@cindex debugger, start-up files of a folder
The original started the debugger in the program's folder, where pdb,
jdb and (under your auto-load setting) gdb ran that folder's
@file{.pdbrc}, @file{jdb.ini} or @file{.gdbinit}, and gdb a script
beside the program (@file{@var{program}-gdb.py}), unseen; handed it a
program named like an option as an option (a file @file{-command=x.c}
made gdb read commands from @file{x.e}); and wrote the folder's
@code{Arguments}, and a project file's watches and breakpoints, into the
debugger's commands as they were, a line break included.  Now the start
shows such a file with its lines, and a folder's @code{Arguments} as the
run command they make, and asks once per folder and text; the program
is always a file; and a value with a line break, or with pdb's
@samp{;;}, is never sent (@pxref{Debugger start-up files}).  gdb starts
in an empty folder of its own and moves to the program's folder after its
start-up, and gdb's auto-load trust inside xwpe is gdb's own default, not
what your @file{~/.gdbinit} set: a script of the folder, of the program or
of a library the program loads runs only when the box showed it and you chose
@samp{Run it}.  pdb and jdb start in an empty folder too: the folder's
@file{.pdbrc} and @file{jdb.ini} are sent by xwpe after @samp{Run it}, a
@file{pdb.py} of the folder cannot replace pdb.  The reason: a debugger reads these
files and lines as its own commands, @code{shell} included, and a list
of what it reads is always one place behind.

@item Where a build writes, and the objects it removes
@cindex Exedir, outside the folder
@cindex EXENAME, outside the folder
The original wrote the program and the objects wherever a folder's
@code{Exedir} or a project file's @code{EXENAME} and @code{LIBNAME}
said, @file{~/.local/bin} included, removed a stale object there before
compiling it, and filled @samp{$(@var{NAME})} in a project file from any
variable of the environment.  Now F9 asks once when a folder's value
puts a build's output, or the project file itself, outside the folder of
the file that set it (links followed, and never counting the home folder
as that folder: a project kept there builds beside itself only its own
objects and program, new or made by an earlier build), never removes an object a
folder's value put outside that folder (your own @code{Exedir}'s objects
are removed as before), and takes
only @code{HOME}, @code{USER}, @code{LOGNAME}, @code{PWD} and
@code{TMPDIR} from the environment for a project's
@samp{$(@var{NAME})}.  The reason: a cloned folder could otherwise write
or delete any file you can, or put a key you hold on a compiler's
command line, on your first F9.
@end table

@section Paths and names

@table @asis
@item A long name or folder
@cindex long file names
@cindex path length
The original copied file names, folders and the text of a dialog field
into buffers of 80, 128 or 256 bytes.  A file name over 127 characters
picked in the file manager, a folder name over about 250 characters in its
tree, Show Definition in such a file, and 128 characters typed into the
text field of Find, Replace, Grep or Show Definition all ran past a buffer
and could end the editor.  xwpe takes any name and folder the system
allows, and a dialog field takes what it shows and no more.  A path from a
project file or an option file that the system itself cannot open (longer
than 4096 bytes) is refused with a box that names it, or skipped and said.
The reason: a cut path names another file.

@item A path that was cut
The original also cut paths it kept for later and used the piece that was
left: a project's @code{LIBNAME} was cut to 79 characters and its
@code{EXENAME} to 128 when @samp{Project-Options} saved them into your
@file{.prj}, and Move, Copy and Link offered the first 128 characters of a
file name and renamed the file to them.  xwpe keeps each of these whole.  The @samp{Executable}, @samp{LiBrary}, @samp{EXecution-Directory},
@samp{System-Include-Path} and @samp{Info-Path} fields take 4095
characters.  The reason: a cut path names another file.

@item An absolute Executable
@cindex Executable, absolute
The original linked a language's @code{Executable} such as
@file{/tmp/prog} below the output folder (@file{.//tmp/prog}) and then ran
@file{/tmp/prog}, another file, which the link had not written.  Run now
starts the file the link wrote.  The reason: F9 and Ctrl-F9 name one
file, and nothing new is written outside the folder.

@item The Executable field after a build
@cindex EXENAME, rewritten by a build
@cindex Project-Options, Executable
The original put the path of the built program into the project's
@samp{Executable} field after F9, so for a project with no @code{EXENAME}
and an @code{Exedir} of @file{out}, @samp{Project-Options} showed
@file{out/a.out}, Save wrote it into the @file{.prj} as @code{EXENAME},
and the next build linked @file{out/out/a.out}.  The field now keeps the
word the file holds, or
offers @code{a.out} when it has none.  The reason: what you typed is never
replaced by a path the editor worked out.

@item What the build trusts as current
@cindex up to date, object and program
@cindex nothing to build, a clone
The original, like make, took any object, program or library newer than its
source as up to date, a clone's shipped one included, so the first F9 in a
clone built nothing and Ctrl-F9 ran the program the clone shipped.  F9's own
compile and link now trust an object, a program or a library only after xwpe
built it in this folder (the record is in your state folder, never in the
project); the first F9 or Ctrl-F9 in a fresh clone, or in a tree you built
elsewhere, compiles and links once, then ``nothing to build'' works as
before.  No question is asked.  A library xwpe did not build in this folder (a shipped
one, or one made by make or by hand) is removed and made anew from the objects
xwpe built, so a member that is not one of the project's files is dropped.  A Makefile build keeps make's rule, and the
classes javac reads from the class path are the compiler's.  The reason: a
clone can ship a built program that is not the program of its sources.
@end table

@section Project lists

@table @asis
@item The blank row at the end of a list
@cindex blank row, project lists
@cindex project lists
The Project, Variable and Install lists end in a blank row where the
cursor stands to add an entry.  The original treated that row as an
entry: @key{Return} or @kbd{Alt-E} on it opened a window named with one
blank, @key{Delete} could remove it, and a Save wrote it into the
@file{.prj} as a blank variable, a blank word in @samp{FILES=} and a blank
@samp{install:} command, and added an @samp{install:} section to a project
that had none.  xwpe says ``No entry in this list'' on the blank row,
keeps it when you delete, and writes none of it.  The reason: a blank row
is where an entry goes, not an entry.
@end table

@section Colours on an X display

@table @asis
@item A full colormap
@cindex colormap, full
@cindex -pcmap
The original stopped at start with ``all colorcells allocated'' on a
palette (8-bit) display whose shared colormap other programs had filled,
unless it was started with @option{-pcmap}.  It now takes, for each
colour it cannot get, the nearest colour already in the colormap when one
is close enough to read as the same; when none is, it switches to a
private colormap by itself, as @option{-pcmap} does, and says so once on
stderr.  The reason: running with nearly the right colours is better than
not running.

@item A colour name the server does not know
The original stopped at start when an X resource named a colour the
server does not know.  It now reports the name on stderr and uses the
built-in colour for that entry, so one typing mistake costs one colour.

@item A regular expression hit
@cindex regular expression, extent of a hit
In the original, a regular expression without a group matched from where
the search began to the end of the row, so @code{a} on @samp{abc} put the
cursor at the end of the row, Replace replaced the rest of the row, and
@code{^} and @code{$} never matched.  The hit is now the text the
expression matched, and @code{^} and @code{$} anchor it to the ends of
the row (@code{^a}, @code{c$}).  A hit always has text in it, so an
expression that matches only empty text (@code{^} or @code{x*} alone)
finds nothing.  The reason: a hit that is not what the user
typed is not a search, and an empty hit would leave Search Again and
Change All standing on it for ever.
@end table
